@page
@model WorkLedger.Pages.FlowModel

<section>
    <h1>Flow animations</h1>
    <p>
        This page hosts the animated cards that narrate the wiring for startup, persistence, API, observability, and tests.
        Each card has a tooltip describing what files or services are touched so you can follow the runtime story without opening an editor.
    </p>
</section>

<div class="code-overlay" id="flowOverlay"></div>

@section Scripts {
<script>
(function () {
    const overlay = document.getElementById("flowOverlay");
    const slides = document.querySelectorAll(".code-slide");

    const closeExpanded = () => {
        slides.forEach((slide) => slide.classList.remove("expanded"));
        overlay.classList.remove("visible");
        const prevScroll = parseFloat(overlay.dataset.prevScroll);
        overlay.dataset.prevScroll = "";
        if (!Number.isNaN(prevScroll)) {
            window.scrollTo({ top: prevScroll, behavior: "smooth" });
        }
    };

    slides.forEach((slide) => {
        slide.addEventListener("dblclick", () => {
            const expanded = slide.classList.toggle("expanded");
            overlay.classList.toggle("visible", expanded);
            if (expanded) {
                overlay.dataset.prevScroll = String(window.scrollY);
                slide.scrollIntoView({ behavior: "smooth", block: "center" });
            } else {
                closeExpanded();
            }
        });
    });

    overlay.addEventListener("click", closeExpanded);
})();
</script>
}

<section class="code-carousel">
    <div class="code-slide hint" data-hint="Program.cs registers the EF Core context, repository, log store, and service in the DI container.">
        <strong>Startup wiring</strong>
        <pre><code>builder.Services.AddDbContext&lt;WorkLedgerDbContext&gt;(opts => opts.UseSqlite("Data Source=workledger.db"));
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;IWorkItemRepository, EfWorkItemRepository&gt;();
builder.Services.AddSingleton&lt;ILogStore, LogStore&gt;();
builder.Services.AddScoped&lt;WorkItemService&gt;();</code></pre>
        <span>Services are registered so Razor PageModels resolve WorkItemService plus the persistence/logging back ends.</span>
        <p class="code-detail">In C#, dependency injection lives in Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection; `AddDbContext` registers the EF `WorkLedgerDbContext`, and the repository/service lifetimes ensure each request shares the same service.</p>
    </div>
        <div class="code-slide hint" data-hint="WorkItemService.cs orchestrates logging, epository calls, and the LTS message bucket for Observability (ILogStore).">
            <strong>Service + logging</strong>
            <pre><code>var message = $"Creating work item '{item.Title}'.";
_logger.LogInformation(message);
_logStore.Append(message);
_repository.Add(item);</code></pre>
        <span>Every CRUD method now writes to ILogger and ILogStore before mutating the repository.</span>
        <p class="code-detail">Each helper formulates a descriptive message, emits it through `ILogger<WorkItemService>`, then writes to the thread-safe `LogStore` before delegating to the repository.</p>
    </div>
        <div class="code-slide hint" data-hint="Data/EfWorkItemRepository.cs implements SaveChanges() for SQLite, while Persistence.cshtml explains the wiring.">
            <strong>Persistence wiring</strong>
            <pre><code>context.WorkItems.Add(item);
context.SaveChanges();</code></pre>
        <span>The EF repository touches the DbContext and commits straight to the SQLite file.</span>
        <p class="code-detail">In C#, `DbSet<WorkItem>` maps to a table; calling `SaveChanges()` pushes the entity state through SQLite using parameterized SQL generated by EF Core.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="code-slide hint" data-hint="Program.cs exposes minimal API endpoints that talk to WorkItemService so external clients can call the same logic as the UI.">
        <strong>API surface</strong>
        <pre><code>app.MapGet("/api/items", (WorkItemService service) => Results.Ok(service.ListItems()));
app.MapPost("/api/items", (WorkItemService service, WorkItem item) =>
{
    service.CreateItem(item);
    return Results.Created($"/api/items/{item.Id}", item);
});</code></pre>
        <span>The API uses the service layer so controllers and pages share the same behavior.</span>
        <p class="code-detail">Minimal APIs accept a `WorkItem` payload and route parameters, reuse `WorkItemService`, and return `IResult` responses (Created, NoContent, NotFound) to mirror HTTP semantics.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="code-slide hint" data-hint="dotnet test / WorkLedger.Tests runs equation so you can verify the service/repository before shipping.">
        <strong>Testing flow</strong>
        <pre><code>dotnet test WorkLedger.Tests</code></pre>
        <span>xUnit hits both the EF repository and the in-memory service to prove CRUD behavior.</span>
        <p class="code-detail">These tests decorate methods with `[Fact]`, spin up repositories/services, execute CRUD flows, and assert expectations using built-in `Assert` helpers.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="code-slide hint" data-hint="The Observability view reads from ILogStore so you can replay the latest log entries.">
        <strong>Observability</strong>
        <pre><code>var entries = _logStore.GetEntries();
foreach (var entry in entries)
{
    Console.WriteLine(entry.Message);
}</code></pre>
        <span>The log store contains the latest CRUD messages, enabling the Observability page to replay them.</span>
        <p class="code-detail">`ILogStore` maintains a linked list of `LogEntry` records, keeping the most recent messages for Observability to render with UTC timestamps.</p>
    </div>
</section>
